from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'dist'
A='https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100a-real-analysis-fall-2020/'
L='https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-06-linear-algebra-spring-2010/'
D='https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-03-differential-equations-spring-2010/'
nodes=[];edges=[]
def add(key,title,level,course,summary,detail,proof,parents,url=''):
 id='college:'+key
 nodes.append(dict(id=id,kind='Concept',title=title,level=level,course=course,summary=summary,detail=detail,proof=proof,url=url,uses=0,extraction='Curated learning bridge. Level is approximate; the example explains an idea, not the connected research claim. Course links are background reading, not sources for the research proof.'))
 for p in parents:edges.append(dict(source=id,target=p,type='LEARNING_PREREQUISITE',evidence='Suggested preparation for understanding this concept; not an asserted proof dependency.'))
 return id
add('determinants','Determinants and nonvanishing','College','Linear algebra','A determinant detects whether a square matrix is invertible.','For a 2×2 matrix [[a,b],[c,d]], det=ad−bc. Nonzero determinant means the associated linear system has a unique solution for every right-hand side.','If ad−bc≠0, multiplying the matrix by [[d,−b],[−c,a]] gives (ad−bc)I. Dividing gives an inverse. For [[1,2],[3,4]], det=−2≠0.',['college:matmul'],L)
add('valuation','Prime valuations of rational numbers','Upper college','Elementary number theory','v_p(q) counts the exponent of a prime p in a nonzero rational q.','Write q as a ratio of integers and subtract denominator exponents from numerator exponents. Negative valuations mean powers of p in the denominator.','For q=12/5=2²·3·5⁻¹, v₂(q)=2, v₃(q)=1, and v₅(q)=−1. Unique prime factorization gives v_p(ab)=v_p(a)+v_p(b) for nonzero rationals a,b.',['concept:prime','concept:rational'])
add('product','The product formula over rational numbers','Upper college','Elementary number theory','The ordinary size of a nonzero rational balances all its prime-based sizes.','Define |q|_p=p^(−v_p(q)). For nonzero rational q, |q| times the product of |q|_p over all primes is one; only finitely many factors differ from one.','Write q=±∏p p^eₚ. Then |q|=∏p p^eₚ and ∏p|q|_p=∏p p^(−eₚ). Multiplying cancels each exponent. This elementary identity does not establish the manuscript’s estimates for each prime.',['college:valuation'])
add('logs','Logarithms turn products into sums','College','Calculus','Logarithms let us compare multiplicative growth using additive estimates.','For positive a,b, log(ab)=log a+log b. A real logarithm requires a strictly positive argument, so log|Δ| requires Δ≠0.','If a_N=e^(−cN²), then log(a_N)/N²=−c. Normalizing a logarithm can expose the rate hidden in a very small number. This is an example, not an estimate for the manuscript determinants.',['concept:functions','concept:limits'],A)
add('limsup','Upper and lower limits','Upper college','Real analysis','Limsup and liminf record eventual upper and lower behavior even without convergence.','For a bounded sequence x_n, take the supremum and infimum of each tail, then let the tail move outward.','For x_n=(−1)^n+1/n, even terms tend to 1 and odd terms tend to −1. Thus limsup=1 and liminf=−1: a sequence can have bounds without a single limit.',['concept:limits','concept:inequalities'],A)
add('catalan-estimates','Why the Catalan argument needs two estimates','Research bridge','Number theory and analysis','The source compares arithmetic and analytic bounds for the same nonzero determinants.','The arithmetic side uses rationality, prime valuations, and a product formula. The analytic side estimates the real size. Normalized logarithms put both on a common scale. Each source estimate remains a separate unverified research claim.','The final numerical comparison is elementary: −2.29084 exceeds −2.2909 by 0.00006. If the claimed lower and upper limits hold for the same real sequence, they contradict liminf≤limsup. The hard work is establishing those estimates.',['college:product','college:logs','college:limsup','college:determinants'])
add('matmul','Matrix multiplication as composition','College','Linear algebra','Matrix multiplication combines two linear transformations.','If A is m×k and B is k×n, then (AB)ᵢⱼ=Σₗ Aᵢₗ Bₗⱼ. Matching inner dimensions ensures the sum makes sense.','For a vector x, (A(Bx))ᵢ=Σₗ AᵢₗΣⱼ Bₗⱼxⱼ=Σⱼ(Σₗ AᵢₗBₗⱼ)xⱼ=(ABx)ᵢ. The equality uses rearrangement of finite sums.',['concept:linear'],L)
add('bilinear','Bilinear maps','Upper college','Linear algebra','A bilinear map is linear in each of two inputs when the other is held fixed.','Matrix multiplication is bilinear: (A+C)B=AB+CB and A(B+C)=AB+AC, with compatible dimensions. Bilinear does not mean jointly linear.','For scalars f(x,y)=xy, f(x+x′,y)=xy+x′y and f(x,y+y′)=xy+xy′. But f(2x,2y)=4xy, so treating the pair as one input does not give a linear map.',['college:matmul'],L)
add('tensor','Counting products in bilinear algorithms','Advanced bridge','Algebraic complexity','A bilinear algorithm expresses outputs as combinations of products of linear forms in the inputs.','Each term multiplies one linear combination of entries of A by one linear combination of entries of B. A tensor records the coefficients. Rank and limiting decompositions are different notions; advanced bounds need more than counting ordinary operations.','A dot product Σᵢaᵢbᵢ is already a sum of coordinate products. Reusing carefully chosen products can sometimes reduce work in larger bilinear maps. This example establishes neither an optimal rank nor the repository’s exponent bound.',['college:bilinear','college:complexity'])
add('complexity','Operation counts and asymptotic growth','College','Algorithms and discrete mathematics','An operation count measures how arithmetic work grows with input size.','The usual n×n multiplication computes n² entries, each with n multiplications and n−1 additions. This is O(n³) arithmetic operations, ignoring bit lengths.','There are n³ multiplications and n²(n−1) additions. Their sum is 2n³−n²≤2n³ for n≥1. Thus the school algorithm is O(n³). This is an upper bound, not a lower bound for all algorithms.',['college:matmul','concept:counting'])
add('exponent','What a matrix multiplication exponent means','Research bridge','Algebraic complexity','The exponent describes the best asymptotic power for square matrix multiplication in a specified arithmetic model.','An exponent bound ω≤α allows algorithms with Oε(n^(α+ε)) operations for each ε>0. The infimum need not itself be attained. The catalogue’s stronger claims are not verified here.','Dividing an n×n product into eight half-size products gives T(n)=8T(n/2)+O(n²). At depth j there are 8ʲ problems of size n/2ʲ; for n=2ᵏ the leaf count is 8ᵏ=n³. Saving recursive products is the motivation, not a proof of a particular improved exponent.',['college:tensor'])
add('partial','Partial derivatives and the chain rule','College','Multivariable calculus','Partial derivatives vary one input while holding the others fixed.','For differentiable u(t,x) along a differentiable curve x(t), d/dt u(t,x(t))=u_t+x′(t)u_x. In several spatial dimensions use a dot product with the gradient.','Take u(t,x)=t+x² and x(t)=t². Direct differentiation of t+t⁴ gives 1+4t³. The chain rule gives 1+(2t)(2t²), the same result.',['concept:derivative'],D)
add('characteristics','Transport along characteristic curves','Upper college','Differential equations','Some PDEs become ODEs when followed along suitable curves.','For u_t+c u_x=0 with constant c, choose x(t)=x₀+ct. The chain rule gives d/dt u(t,x(t))=0.','Thus u(t,x₀+ct)=u(0,x₀). Writing x₀=x−ct yields u(t,x)=u₀(x−ct) for differentiable initial data. The nonlinear coupled kinetic system requires additional analysis.',['college:partial','concept:differential'],D)
add('integrating-factor','Integrating factors','College','Differential equations','Multiplication by a chosen function can turn a first-order equation into a product derivative.','For y′=ay+b(t), with constant a and continuous b, multiply by e^(−at).','The product rule gives (e^(−at)y)′=e^(−at)(y′−ay)=e^(−at)b(t). Integrate from 0 to t and multiply by e^(at) to obtain y(t)=e^(at)[y(0)+∫₀ᵗe^(−as)b(s)ds].',['concept:derivative','concept:integral'],D)
add('gronwall','A basic differential Grönwall estimate','Upper college','Differential equations and analysis','A differential inequality can bound growth without solving the original equation.','If E is differentiable, E′≤CE, C is constant, and t≥0, then E(t)≤E(0)e^(Ct).','Differentiate F=e^(−Ct)E. Then F′=e^(−Ct)(E′−CE)≤0. Hence F(t)≤F(0), which gives the bound. For PDE use, one must first justify the inequality and all differentiations.',['college:integrating-factor','concept:inequalities'],D)
add('energy','Energy estimates as a method','Advanced bridge','Partial differential equations','Track an integral quantity to control a solution over time.','Which norm is controlled matters: finite energy alone need not bound all derivatives or guarantee a smooth solution. Boundary behavior and regularity must justify integration by parts.','Toy example: a smooth periodic solution of u_t=u_xx has E(t)=½∫u² dx. Differentiating and integrating by parts gives E′=∫u u_xx dx=−∫u_x² dx≤0. This is a heat-equation example, not a Vlasov–Maxwell estimate.',['college:partial','concept:integral','college:gronwall'])
add('kinetic','From particle transport to smoothness questions','Research bridge','Kinetic PDE','A particle distribution is transported by fields that it also helps generate.','The relativistic Vlasov–Maxwell system couples transport in position and momentum with electromagnetic field equations. Global smoothness requires control strong enough to continue the solution; the catalogue’s claimed theorem is not checked here.','In the simpler free-transport equation f_t+v·∇ₓf=0 with fixed v, f(t,x,v)=f₀(x−tv,v). Differentiating verifies the equation. The actual coupled system adds evolving momentum and fields, so this example supplies intuition only.',['college:characteristics','college:energy'])
paths=[dict(family='f005',title='Catalan → determinants, primes and limits',start='college:catalan-estimates'),dict(family='f107',title='Matrix multiplication → linear algebra',start='college:exponent'),dict(family='f362',title='Global smoothness → calculus and ODEs',start='college:kinetic')]
for p in paths:edges.append(dict(source=p['family'],target=p['start'],type='SUGGESTED_BACKGROUND',evidence='Problem-specific learning route; not a complete proof reduction.'))
for id,target in [('reason:lower','college:product'),('reason:upper','college:logs'),('reason:nonzero','college:valuation'),('reason:bounds','college:limsup')]:edges.append(dict(source=id,target=target,type='LEARNING_PREREQUISITE',evidence='Background for reading this source-dependent proof contract.'))
for n in nodes:n['uses']=sum(e['target']==n['id'] for e in edges)
(R/'paths.json').write_text(json.dumps(dict(nodes=nodes,edges=edges,paths=paths),ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
